(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-micro-exam-2-webster-review-quiz"]={"title":"Webster Review Quiz 1 — Microbiology Exam 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Of the four immunopathologies, which two are classified as underreactions of the immune system?","choices":["Allergy and immunodeficiency","Immunodeficiency and cancer","Autoimmunity and cancer","Allergy and autoimmunity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: immunodeficiency is a deficiency or loss of immunity, and cancer is both a cause and an effect of immune dysfunction; both are classed as underreactions. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Free antibody binds a soluble antigen, and the immune complexes embed in basement membranes and start an inflammatory reaction. Which condition is an example of this hypersensitivity?","choices":["Serum sickness","Hay fever","Transfusion reaction","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: free immunoglobulin G or M binding soluble antigen into immune complexes that embed in basement membranes is type III, and serum sickness is its example, as is lupus. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which feature sets type I hypersensitivity apart from types II and III?","choices":["It needs a large amount of antigen","It is initiated by immunoglobulin G and M","It is mediated by T cells, not antibody","Its antibody is already bound to cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in type I the immunoglobulin E is cell bound, on mast cells, basophils and eosinophils, and allergen crosslinks it; in types II and III free antibody binds the antigen. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A person with pollen allergy receives a course of subcutaneous allergen injections and afterward reacts far less to pollen. What do the injections chiefly stimulate?","choices":["A halt to all immunoglobulin E production","More immunoglobulin E bound to mast cells","Antibody that destroys the mast cells","Immunoglobulin G that blocks the allergen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: injected allergen may stimulate high levels of allergen-specific immunoglobulin G, which blocks the allergen from the immunoglobulin E on mast cells, so they do not degranulate. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient whose red cells carry neither the A nor the B antigen needs a red cell transfusion. Which donor red cells are compatible?","choices":["Type B","Type AB","Type A","Type O"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a recipient lacking both antigens carries antibodies against both A and B in the serum, so only type O red cells, which carry neither antigen, escape them. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"An Rh-negative woman's first pregnancy, with an Rh-positive fetus, ended without problems, and she received no Rhogam. Her second fetus is also Rh-positive. Why is this fetus at risk of hemolytic disease?","choices":["She has made anti-Rh antibody since birth","The first pregnancy sensitized her to Rh","The fetus makes antibody against her cells","The father's Rh-positive cells sensitized her"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: with an Rh-negative mother and an Rh-positive fetus, the first pregnancy is normally fine but sensitizes her, and her anti-Rh antibody can make later pregnancies hemolytic. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 36-37"},{"q":"Which pairing of a transplant problem with its hypersensitivity type is correct?","choices":["Graft versus host disease: type III","Acute rejection: type II","Chronic rejection: type III","Hyperacute rejection: type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: chronic rejection, months or years after transplantation, is a type III reaction; hyperacute rejection is type II, acute is type IV, and graft versus host disease is type IV. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 44-47"},{"q":"An autoimmune disease is being classified by its mechanism of tissue damage. Which mechanism can be ruled out?","choices":["Immunoglobulin E crosslinked on mast cells","T cells destroying the target tissue","Antibody against cell-surface receptors","Immune complexes in basement membranes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: autoimmunity involves every hypersensitivity type except type I, so immunoglobulin E crosslinked on mast cells, the type I mechanism, plays no part. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 52, 60-61"},{"q":"Which patient's immunodeficiency is classified as primary?","choices":["An infant born with adenosine deaminase deficiency","A patient with a blood cell cancer","A patient treated with radiation therapy","An adult infected with human immunodeficiency virus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a primary immunodeficiency is congenital, usually a genetic error, and adenosine deaminase deficiency is one of the primary defects, blocking T cells from giving cell-mediated immunity. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 62-65"},{"q":"A tumor's cells carry an antigen that the patient's normal cells also carry, in smaller amounts. How is this antigen classified?","choices":["Sequestered antigen","Tumor-specific antigen","Tumor-associated antigen","Cross-reactive antigen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: tumor-associated antigens are present on tumor cells and on normal cells in smaller amounts, and they provide targets for immune defense against cancer cells in a healthy person. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 70, 53"},{"q":"A monoclonal antibody against a lymphoma cell marker is linked to a chemotherapy drug. What is the main benefit of the linkage?","choices":["It engineers T cells outside the body","It carries the drug to the tumor cells","It activates natural killer and dendritic cells","It blocks the checkpoint off signal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the antibody binds the cell carrying its marker and takes the drug with it; inside the lymphoma cell the drug binds microtubules, no mitotic spindle forms and the cell dies. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 76-77"},{"q":"Which form of anthrax first appears as ulceration of the mouth and esophagus, followed by localized lymphadenopathy?","choices":["Injection","Cutaneous","Pulmonary","Gastrointestinal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: gastrointestinal anthrax follows eating contaminated meat; it begins with ulcers of the mouth and esophagus and localized lymphadenopathy, then nausea, vomiting and malaise if the upper intestine is invaded. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 10-14"},{"q":"Which route of exposure to Bacillus anthracis spores leads to the most deadly form of anthrax?","choices":["Inhaling spores from animal products","Injecting contaminated heroin","Spores entering a cut in the skin","Eating meat from an infected animal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: spores inhaled from infected animal products or soil cause pulmonary anthrax (woolsorters' disease), the most deadly form; untreated, it kills within about three days of the first symptoms. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 10, 12-14"},{"q":"A Gram-positive rod carries a round spore at one end that swells the cell. Which oxygen requirement fits its genus?","choices":["Strictly aerobic","Aerotolerant or anaerobic","Obligately anaerobic","Aerobic or facultatively anaerobic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a spore at one end that swells the rod is the Clostridium pattern (Clostridium tetani), and the genus is anaerobic, the obligate anaerobes of the differentiation scheme, making spores only without oxygen. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 5, 8, 19, 28, 65, 67"},{"q":"Gas gangrene begins when Clostridium perfringens spores reach a wound. Where are these spores found?","choices":["Droplets from healthy carriers","Products of infected animals","Honey and low-acid home-canned foods","Soil, human skin, intestine and vagina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the spores of the myonecrosis clostridia are found in soil, on human skin and in the intestine and vagina, so surgical incisions, compound fractures and other wounds are easily contaminated. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 13, 21, 34, 38, 59, 61"},{"q":"Myonecrosis develops in a mixed infection. Which organisms make up the mix?","choices":["Spores and vegetative cells of one species","Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria","Two Clostridium species growing together","Aerobic bacteria with the anaerobic clostridia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: aerobic bacteria in the wound use up the oxygen, producing the low oxygen tension that lets the anaerobic clostridial spores germinate and grow. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 21, 22"},{"q":"After entering at motor nerve endings, where does tetanospasmin block regulatory function?","choices":["Intestinal epithelial cells","Spinal inhibitory neurons","The neuromuscular junction","Cells of the heart muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the toxin goes to the spinal cord and blocks the regulatory function of the spinal inhibitory neurons, stopping release of gamma-aminobutyric acid and glycine, so muscles contract uncontrollably. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 29, 30, 35, 41, 62"},{"q":"Foodborne botulism is an intoxication. What has the patient swallowed?","choices":["Cells that invade the gut wall","Spores that germinate in the gut","Toxin already made in the food","Cells that make toxin in the gut"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: spores left on poorly preserved food germinate in its anaerobic conditions and release botulin there, so foodborne botulism is poisoning by toxin already present when the food is eaten. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 38, 41"},{"q":"Before it causes disease, where does Clostridioides difficile normally live?","choices":["In the skin's pilosebaceous glands","In soil, entering through wounds","In the colon, in low numbers","In poorly preserved canned food"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is a normal resident of the colon in low numbers and relatively non-invasive; disease follows when broad-spectrum antibiotics kill the competing bacteria and it overgrows. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 21, 28, 34, 42, 65"},{"q":"Which patient is at greatest risk of Clostridioides difficile infection?","choices":["A healthy adult who ate soft cheese","A worker handling infected animal products","An adult with a deep puncture wound","An older inpatient on antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: antibiotic exposure, long stays in healthcare settings and advanced age are listed risks; broad-spectrum antibiotics kill the competing flora so the colon resident overgrows. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 13, 29, 42, 45, 52, 55"},{"q":"Besides cold, which conditions is Listeria monocytogenes resistant to?","choices":["Phagocytes, through a capsule","Heat, salt, pH extremes and bile","Lysosomes, through cord factor","Antibiotics, through a biofilm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Listeria is resistant to cold, heat, salt, pH extremes and bile, yet pasteurization and cooking are still its prevention. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 9, 53, 56, 66, 70"},{"q":"Respiratory diphtheria runs in two stages. What happens in the second stage?","choices":["Bacilli spread to kidneys and long bones","Spores germinate in the airway","Toxemia damages the heart and nerves","A membrane forms over the throat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: after the local infection, diphtherotoxin production and toxemia target the heart (myocarditis, abnormal electrocardiogram) and the nerves (muscle weakness and paralysis). — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 59, 62, 75"},{"q":"Beyond acne, what is Cutibacterium acnes now implicated in?","choices":["Deep wound gas gangrene","Meningitis in newborns","Implant and shunt infections","Pseudomembranous colitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is more recently implicated in chronic invasive, implant, endovascular and central nervous system shunt infections, helped by a biofilm on prosthetics. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 21-23, 42, 43, 55, 66"},{"q":"A patient fails to recover from primary tuberculosis; tubercles expand and drain into the bronchial tubes, with violent coughing and bloody sputum. Which stage is this?","choices":["Secondary tuberculosis","Primary tuberculosis","Inactive tuberculosis","Disseminated tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: when the patient does not recover from primary tuberculosis the bacilli reactivate, tubercles expand and drain into the bronchial tubes, and violent coughing, greenish or bloody sputum, weight loss and fatigue follow. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 72-75"},{"q":"Leprosy begins in the skin and mucous membranes. Into which tissue does it then progress?","choices":["The colon wall","The lungs","The nerves","Deep muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: leprosy is a chronic disease that begins in the skin and mucous membranes and progresses into the nerves; untreated bacilli grow in skin macrophages and the Schwann cells of peripheral nerves. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 12, 42, 72, 73, 80, 83"},{"q":"Where is Actinomyces israelii a normally harmless resident?","choices":["Mouth, digestive and genital tracts","Pilosebaceous glands of the skin","The airways of healthy carriers","Soil and animal intestines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Actinomyces israelii is a normally harmless resident of the oral cavity, digestive tract and genital tract; it causes chronic, hard-to-diagnose infection when it reaches deeper tissue. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 28, 55, 61, 65, 93"},{"q":"Which kinds of infection does Nocardia cause?","choices":["Pulmonary, cutaneous or subcutaneous","Gastrointestinal, injection or meningeal","Pharyngeal, tonsillar or laryngeal","Cervicofacial, thoracic or uterine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Nocardia, a filamentous bacterium, causes pulmonary, cutaneous or subcutaneous infections and is also involved in mycetoma of the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 12-15, 61, 93, 94"},{"q":"Which organism is spread by respiratory droplets from healthy carriers as well as from actively infected people?","choices":["Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Listeria monocytogenes","Mycobacterium leprae","Mycobacterium tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: diphtheria has a reservoir of healthy carriers and is acquired via respiratory droplets from carriers or actively infected individuals, so the potential is always present. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 55, 61, 71, 72, 82"},{"q":"A community health survey records the age, neighborhood and month of onset of each resident's rash illness, to see how the illness is distributed by person, place and time. What can a study of this kind yield?","choices":["The cause in each person","Hypotheses and associations","A cause-effect relationship","A tested specific hypothesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a community health survey is descriptive epidemiology, which examines distribution by person, place and time and formulates hypotheses and examines associations. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 32, 43"},{"q":"An epidemiologist helps a state health department set up, manage and evaluate a system that routinely collects, analyzes and interprets data on a disease. Which use of epidemiological training is this?","choices":["Public health communication","Experimental epidemiology","Public health surveillance","Outbreak response"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: setting up, managing and evaluating public health surveillance systems, which collect, analyze and interpret disease data, is one of the uses of epidemiological training. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 7, 8, 14"},{"q":"Genomic epidemiology monitors the genetic makeup of the pathogens that cause disease. What made it possible?","choices":["Spot maps of cases","Koch's postulates","Life-table approaches","Advances in sequencing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: genomic epidemiology, the monitoring of the genetic makeup of disease-causing pathogens, was made possible by advances in sequencing techniques. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 41, 42, 69"},{"q":"Investigators want epidemiologic data gathered directly from individual persons. Which collection method fits that source?","choices":["Questionnaires and surveys","Sales and court records","River water and soil samples","Notifications of observed cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: data from individual persons are collected by questionnaire or survey, as in a foodborne illness outbreak or a national health and nutrition survey. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A country's routine health information system relies on the reports that health care facilities and providers submit. Which type of surveillance is this?","choices":["Active surveillance","Syndromic surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Passive surveillance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: passive surveillance relies on reports submitted by health care facilities and providers, and it includes routine health information systems. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 19-20"},{"q":"Which reportable disease belongs to the group that requires a mandatory written report?","choices":["Chickenpox","Gonorrhea","Pertussis","Cancer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: gonorrhea and salmonellosis are the examples of reportable diseases in the group that requires a mandatory written report. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A respiratory infection arises at the same rate in two cities. In City B it causes no deaths, yet its prevalence there is far lower. Which explanation fits?","choices":["Patients in City B live longer uncured","Patients in City B recover sooner","Patients in City B die sooner","More new cases arise in City B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: prevalence depends on incidence and duration of illness; with equal incidence and no deaths, the lower prevalence in City B points to rapid recovery. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A study links an exposure to a cancer that has a latent period of about 15 years, but in most cases the exposure began only 2 years before diagnosis. Which of Hill's criteria does this timing fail to meet?","choices":["Temporal sequence","Biological gradient","Strength of association","Consistency of findings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: temporal sequence requires exposure to precede disease and, in diseases with latency periods, to precede the latent period; exposure 2 years before diagnosis falls inside a 15-year latency. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 46, 50"},{"q":"A large study finds that a lung disease is twice as common among people living beside busy highways. No study has yet shown that the exposure came before the disease, or that risk rises with traffic exposure. What does this evidence establish so far?","choices":["A causal relationship","An association","A chance finding","Each patient's own cause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: an identifiable relationship between an exposure and a disease is an association; without temporal sequence or a dose-response gradient, causation cannot yet be inferred. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 43, 44, 46, 49, 50"},{"q":"A new respiratory infection spreads uncontrolled through one city's neighborhoods but has not appeared in any other country. Which term fits?","choices":["Pandemic","Epidemic","Sporadic","Endemic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: an epidemic is a disease occurring in excess of what might be expected, or as an uncontrolled spread in a community; it need not be worldwide. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"An epidemic curve of an intestinal infection shows a sharp peak among guests who drank from one punch bowl at a party, followed over the next six weeks by successive waves of cases among people who never attended but had contact with ill guests. Which type of epidemic does this curve show?","choices":["Point common source","Propagated","Continuous common source","Mixed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a mixed epidemic may start as a common source, here the punch bowl, and then be propagated person to person, as the later waves among contacts show. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 55-57"},{"q":"Having chosen a policy, a state health department sets target goals and allocates the money and staff the policy needs. Which step of the policy cycle is this?","choices":["Policy choices","Policy evaluation","Policy implementation","Assessment of interventions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: policy implementation is the step of the policy cycle at which target goals are set and the necessary resources are allocated. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 73"}]};
